{"id":"lm-678258e944342d54cc1352b4","title":"A Learnable Meta Germany","group":"LM Europe","desc":"learnablemeta.com map","cards":[{"q":["https://learnablemeta.com/images/232/pe0sVHQivjpOxgIHPPW7C6tyOkOYDWRNV8TM.avif"],"a":"Alp region","slug":"germany","note":"<p>The German Alps is the most mountainous region in Germany, it is mostly concentrated on the Austrian Border.</p>"},{"q":["https://learnablemeta.com/images/232/EFYnZ8JR8QftDYjkzVcLjQnIvtW6XZvIcau1.avif"],"a":"Black slate wall","slug":"germany","note":"<p>Houses with <strong>black slate walls</strong>, resembling fish scales, are commonly found in a large area of central Germany.</p>"},{"q":["https://learnablemeta.com/images/232/N5Vm3qLqX3sWSFZOBDF2ed8KeK3wmGPPcTp1.avif"],"a":"Brick houses","slug":"germany","note":"<p>These brick houses are generally concentrated in <strong>northwestern</strong> Germany.</p>\n<p>NOTE: This map is very approximate.</p>"},{"q":["https://learnablemeta.com/images/232/qtKpChfJrCiOLE3cQNnJPM2Akm973WcyFX76.avif"],"a":"Commie blocks","slug":"germany","note":"<p>These <strong>large</strong>, utilitarian <strong>apartment buildings</strong> constructed during the socialist era, that are also known as “<a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Urban_planning_in_communist_countries\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\">Commie blocks</a>” are common in all of former East Germany.</p>"},{"q":["https://learnablemeta.com/images/232/aDZhapHLz8TZxtiCEKCy4ILMtAqiskX6avFe.avif"],"a":"Cycling signs","slug":"germany","note":"<p>Different regions of Germany use a slightly different cycling sign design.</p>"},{"q":["https://learnablemeta.com/images/232/NShhVprsu1LbM5drwO0yII8InRhTnUZ6dEF9.avif"],"a":"Dark reflectors","slug":"germany","note":"<p>Bollards with <strong>darker reflectors</strong> than usual are found in these regions.</p>"},{"q":["https://learnablemeta.com/images/232/4nDNHA7IQWLludkLkJueHQ7vRfHxTvXoWbdv.avif"],"a":"Green bollard top","slug":"germany","note":"<p>Some bollards in North Rhine-Westphalia have a <strong>green</strong> bit at the top. The top can also be <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.google.com/maps/@?api=1&#x26;map_action=pano&#x26;pano=9bQ7ghWesD4IbANDTu_Ndg&#x26;viewpoint=50.952102,6.693283&#x26;heading=223.60373118459188&#x26;pitch=-13.614248811957069&#x26;fov=18.349144415107048&#x26;shorturl=1#extra%5Btags%5D=green+cap\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\">another colour</a>.</p>"},{"q":["https://learnablemeta.com/images/232/0PeZFGsTzed5NdiahkFiKIk6z3RJVUCGXvqY.avif"],"a":"Green-LVR-Top","slug":"germany","note":"<p>Some of the green caps will have the text ‘<em>LVR</em>’, along with a small <em>coat of arms</em>. These specific green caps are unique to southern North Rhine-Westphalia.</p>"},{"q":["https://learnablemeta.com/images/232/t9svxEm5EalE955zN1C9vTfOdovrjOUkT52d.avif"],"a":"Guardrail types","slug":"germany","note":"<p>While most of Germany uses <strong>B-Type</strong> guardrails, some regions predominantly use A-Type instead. Learning which regions use them can be useful.</p>\n<p>NOTE: There will always be rare exceptions. For a good overview of European guardrails, see this <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://images.squarespace-cdn.com/content/v1/60f6054f4e76b03092956de8/930bd2e7-57fa-4807-8e8a-733ba5a702a8/europeguardrail.png?format=2500w\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\">infographic</a>.</p>"},{"q":["https://learnablemeta.com/images/232/CAe26jD1j0r2Ldma59FBD4setUPcFtGUvQeR.avif"],"a":"North Munich hop fields","slug":"germany","note":"<p>You can find <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hops\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\">hop</a> fields in these regions. They are commonly seen just north of Munich.</p>"},{"q":["https://learnablemeta.com/images/232/yLpQyZFDe4Fh8yjaGooPXeT6HNQ9vs8Gj1S8.avif"],"a":"Northeast tree marking","slug":"germany","note":"<p>You can spot trees that are painted white close to the bottom of the trunk in the northeast.\n<em>You will rarely find them outside the shaded region</em>.</p>"},{"q":["https://learnablemeta.com/images/232/1EazrZqyBGHBrPGKw0W1AEIzpFAlFF9mQaxX.avif"],"a":"Pines","slug":"germany","note":"<p>Baltic Pines or <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pinus_sylvestris\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\">Pinus sylvestris</a> are generally more concentrated in the <strong>northeast</strong> of Germany. They can be recognized by their orange-tinged trunk.\nNOTE: This map is very approximate.</p>"},{"q":["https://learnablemeta.com/images/232/5AeVbhn9QS6tmIwgB09rsrfMqtKUBWuzg9PN.avif"],"a":"Place name suffixes","slug":"germany","note":"<p>These are some of the more useful and common suffixes you can use to regionguess Germany.</p>"},{"q":["https://learnablemeta.com/images/232/Q55X8trbxuhI2Wf8fRvmrtDq2Xfwa8BDv2Vw.avif"],"a":"Sign back connector","slug":"germany","note":"<p>You can also use the sign back connectors on street signs to regionguess Germany.</p>\n<p>NOTE: The double connector on triangular signs are generally <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.google.com/maps/@?api=1&#x26;map_action=pano&#x26;pano=RusJTKfxDDmFE8fcROnEYA&#x26;viewpoint=53.99698,10.605746&#x26;heading=213.57816760413397&#x26;pitch=0.3299433438978525&#x26;fov=17.531374769215006&#x26;shorturl=1#extra%5Btags%5D=Flat+capital+I&#x26;extra%5BloadMode%5D=latLng\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\">wider</a>. T-shaped connectors may look like <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.google.com/maps/@?api=1&#x26;map_action=pano&#x26;pano=sNlEXVIP_0tHqheca3BZPw&#x26;viewpoint=53.449275,9.970171&#x26;heading=268.9620226716969&#x26;pitch=-1.3648369529043407&#x26;fov=17.531374769215006&#x26;shorturl=1#extra%5Btags%5D=SignBack&#x26;extra%5Btags%5D=T&#x26;extra%5Btags%5D=Flat+capital+I&#x26;extra%5BloadMode%5D=latLng\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\">this</a>.</p>"},{"q":["https://learnablemeta.com/images/232/uDMVzVAWNfBZxXUi7iGSSP80lJ2GU2s8BcV8.avif"],"a":"Sign backs","slug":"germany","note":"<p>You can use the sign backs of street signs to regionguess Germany.</p>\n<p>Here are examples of a <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.google.com/maps/@49.1552364,7.2395117,3a,17.5y,95.01h,82.16t/data=!3m7!1e1!3m5!1sONWnGhtl2llamoZmWabjxg!2e0!6shttps:%2F%2Fstreetviewpixels-pa.googleapis.com%2Fv1%2Fthumbnail%3Fcb_client%3Dmaps_sv.tactile%26w%3D900%26h%3D600%26pitch%3D7.839640000000003%26panoid%3DONWnGhtl2llamoZmWabjxg%26yaw%3D95.0085!7i16384!8i8192?entry=ttu&#x26;g_ep=EgoyMDI1MDExNS4wIKXMDSoASAFQAw%3D%3D\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\">folded</a>, and <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.google.com/maps/@?api=1&#x26;map_action=pano&#x26;pano=bVU-eeFmDLCaV7ZIAz2ocA&#x26;viewpoint=50.584233,12.421672&#x26;heading=93.74301381705851&#x26;pitch=5.275431067575241&#x26;fov=17.531374769215006&#x26;shorturl=1#extra%5Btags%5D=No+fold&#x26;extra%5BloadMode%5D=latLng\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\">flat</a> sign back.</p>"},{"q":["https://learnablemeta.com/images/232/p10OyAfsOVgxWZKthvhf3UT7gvVZIwrVoUkJ.avif"],"a":"Southeast Regensburg flat plains","slug":"germany","note":"<p>The flat plains southeast of <strong>Regensburg</strong> can be recognised by the <strong>slight ridge</strong> often visible to the northeast. The plains themselves are <strong>flat</strong>, and quite <strong>agricultural</strong>.</p>"},{"q":["https://learnablemeta.com/images/232/qgn7KiwkFD6p1HDpjnP3YweguD8H6GYJiEQt.avif"],"a":"Southwest wineyards","slug":"germany","note":"<p>ineyards are mostly concentrated in the <strong>southwest</strong> of Germany. However, there are a couple regions further east as well.</p>"},{"q":["https://learnablemeta.com/images/232/AdJUkxbEXmLTEdwBU7AHUMCvRDprV8IRspSS.avif"],"a":"State flags","slug":"germany","note":"<p>Each German state has their own flag design. Many of them are rare to find, though.</p>"},{"q":["https://learnablemeta.com/images/232/WdRChSBIG5Rj8HFh2dtdcMznpRwaoVrpFqIv.avif"],"a":"Thatched roofs","slug":"germany","note":"<p><strong>Thatched roofs</strong> and <strong>rock walls</strong> are most common on the <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North_Frisian_Islands\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\">North Frisian Islands</a>, as well as mainland Schleswig-Holstein. You can also commonly find them in coastal <strong>resort towns</strong> in Mecklenburg-Vorpommern.</p>"}]}